<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Offa's Dyke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>'s Dyke is a linear earthwork running roughly along the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, generally attributed to Offa, king of Mercia from 757 to 796. Built as a bank with a ditch on its western side, it survives over about 130 kilometres and faced Welsh kingdoms such as Powys. A long-distance footpath now follows its course.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/offas-dyke.jpg" alt="Offa's Dyke" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Heaton · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Offa%27s_Dyke_near_Clun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
